WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.115
Booking an appointment looks like a simple
click. But two people

00:00:03.115 --> 00:00:06.360
may request the same doctor and time. A
reliable system must

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.865
make one correct decision, even when both
screens looked available.

00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:13.855
CareIndex checks the request origin and
session first. Staff need the

00:00:13.855 --> 00:00:17.025
right permission. A patient account is tied
to its own fictional

00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:20.185
profile, so changing a browser field cannot
book for an unrelated

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:21.180
patient.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.735
The booking path begins an immediate SQLite
transaction before checking availability.

00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:30.510
SQLite serialises writers. This prevents two
independent check-then-write sequences from
both

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.420
treating the same slot as free.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.300
A partial unique index allows only one
scheduled booking for a

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.065
doctor, date, and time. The application also
checks working hours and

00:00:39.065 --> 00:00:43.265
existing reservations. The database rule
remains a final protection.

00:00:43.595 --> 00:00:47.120
The appointment and its invoice are created
inside the same transaction.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.785
Both commit together. If something fails, the
transaction rolls back. A

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:55.660
competing request receives a controlled
conflict instead of a second reservation.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.900
The engineering lab explains database
behaviour and separate algorithms, including
A

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.875
V L rotations. It does not claim that a
teaching tree

00:01:02.875 --> 00:01:08.040
replaces SQLite indexing. All patients are
fictional, and payments are simulated.
